# Test DecL — programs harvested from the pytest suite
# =====================================================

# This file extracts every DecL program embedded in the ``tests/`` tree so
# the language coverage exercised by pytest is also available as a flat,
# greppable corpus. It complements ``test_suite.agg`` (broad language
# walkthrough) and ``test_suite2.agg`` (mixed/spliced severities). The
# canonical source of each program is its Python module — this file is a
# reference extraction, not the runtime contract.
#
# Numbering uses fresh letter prefixes (P..X) that do not collide with
# anything in test_suite.agg (A..O) or test_suite2.agg (G splice
# extension). The three files are intended to be combined into a single
# ``test_suite.agg`` at v1.0 release, so the numbering is contiguous.
#
# DecL syntax reminders honoured here:
#   * ``port`` definitions are single-line (sub-agg clauses inlined).
#   * ``note{...}`` is only legal on ``agg`` and ``sev`` lines; for
#     ``dist`` / ``distortion`` we use ``#`` end-of-line comments instead.

# Contents
#
# P. Baseline harness — Aggregate regression cases   (tests/baseline/corpus.py)
# Q. Baseline harness — Portfolio regression cases   (tests/baseline/corpus.py)
# R. PEG regression — two-unit lognorm + gamma mix   (tests/peg.py)
# S. Bounds regression — bounded BDD portfolio       (tests/test_bounds.py)
# T. Splice / layered-severity regressions           (tests/test_splice_suite.py)
# U. Validation regressions — symmetric & low-skew   (tests/test_validation.py)
# V. Underwriter / build smoke fixtures              (tests/test_underwriter.py)
# W. Distortion DecL constructors                    (tests/test_distortion_construction.py)
# X. Parser-error fixtures (intentional typos)       (tests/test_parser_errors_integration.py)


# P. Baseline harness — Aggregate regression cases
# ================================================
# Pinned grids and snapshots in tests/baseline/data/. Each case targets a
# specific path through the FFT pipeline; together they fence the v1.0
# core-compute refactor. See tests/baseline/corpus.py for the grids
# (log2/bs/padding/normalize) bound to each program.
agg P.Base.FixedOne 1 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 0.5 fixed                                                                 note{fixed-1 shortcut path}
agg P.Base.DfreqOne dfreq [1] sev lognorm 100 cv 0.5                                                                     note{full-FFT path equivalent to FixedOne}
agg P.Sym.Dice     dfreq [1] dsev [1:6]                                                                                  note{symmetric discrete; skew dust regression}
agg P.Tail.LN      5 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 3 poisson                                                                 note{thick tail; tail accuracy on wide grid}
agg P.Re.Both    100 claims 5000 xs 0 sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 3500 po 4000 xs 1000 poisson aggregate net of 2000 xs 3000   note{occurrence + aggregate reinsurance}
agg ReBucket      10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 0.8 so 250 xs 175 poisson                              note{off-grid excess attach; reins_bucket linear/nearest rebucketing}
agg P.Def.Pareto   1 claim sev 1000 * pareto 1.5 - 1000 fixed                                                            note{defective; no finite 2nd moment} hints{normalize=False}
agg P.Mix         [10 20] claims [100 400] xs 0 sev lognorm [20 40 100] cv [.5 .6 .7] wts=3 mixed gamma .2                note{bounded mixture, mixed gamma frequency}


# Q. Baseline harness — Portfolio regression cases
# ================================================
# Three portfolios cover (i) unbounded gamma/lognorm with fixed-1 frequency,
# (ii) bounded beta mixture, (iii) bounded discrete (Bodoff). Used to pin
# linear/lifted allocation and the bounded-detection refusal of
# ``allocation='lifted'`` on unbounded + mass distortions.
port Q.Port.CNC     agg CNC.NonCat 25 claim sev gamma 80 cv 0.15 mixed gamma .2 agg CNC.Cat 5 claim 200 xs 0 sev lognorm 40 cv 1.50 mixed ig .2
port Q.Port.Bounded agg A dfreq [1:3] sev 500 * beta 5 2 agg B dfreq [1:5] sev 800 * beta 7 2
port Q.Port.Bodoff  agg wind 1 claim sev dhistogram xps [0, 99] [0.80, 0.20] fixed agg quake 1 claim sev dhistogram xps [0, 100] [0.95, 0.05] fixed


# R. PEG regression — two-unit portfolio
# ======================================
# The calibrate-distortions / pricing-pipeline regression fixture
# (tests/peg.py). Exercises a limit-and-attachment severity, a three-component
# lognorm mixture per unit, and different mixing CVs (0.25 vs 0.35) for the
# mixed-vs-independent correlation case. Five calibrated distortions follow
# (ccoc / ph / wang / dual / tvar). The DecL is the contract — do not change
# without re-capturing ``tests/data/peg_baseline.json``.
port R.PEG agg A 100 claims 50 xs 0 sev lognorm [30 50 100] cv [0.1 .6 1.8] wts [.5 .25 .25] mixed gamma .25 agg B 150 claims 100 xs 0 sev lognorm [30 50 100] cv [0.1 .6 1.8] wts [.5 .25 .25] mixed gamma .35


# S. Bounds regression — BDD portfolio
# ====================================
# Bounded beta severities, fixed-1 frequency: a finite-support portfolio
# where the closed-form anchor (premium = TVaR_0.5(total) → p_star = 0.5)
# lets the ``Bounds`` cloud columns collapse to a known distortion. See
# tests/test_bounds.py.
port S.BDD agg A 1 claim sev 10 * beta 2 3 fixed agg B 1 claim sev 15 * beta 4 2 fixed


# T. Splice / layered-severity regressions
# ========================================
# Pins the analytical moments for spliced + layered compositions
# (tests/test_splice_suite.py). T.Splice01/02 are bare spliced severities;
# T.SpliceLayer01/02 wrap them in layer-and-attachment policies whose
# conditional moments are computable by hand.
sev T.Splice01      lognorm 10 cv .5 splice [20 30]                                           note{single-segment splice; mean and moms finite}
sev T.Splice02 10 * uniform + 5 splice [8 12]                                                 note{shift+scale splice; fz.sf(9) ~ 0.75 splice-only survival}
agg T.SpliceLayer01 1 claim 5 xs 3 sev 10 * uniform + 5 splice [8 12] fixed                   note{full-limit; E[claim^k] = 5^k}
agg T.SpliceLayer02 1 claim 2 xs 8 sev 10 * uniform + 5 splice [8 12] fixed                   note{proper excess; E[claim] = 1.5, E[claim^2] = 8/3, E[claim^3] = 5}
agg T.SpliceUnbounded 5 claims sev lognorm 40 cv .65 splice [1 100] poisson                   note{splice on unbounded base; window must be finite}


# U. Validation regressions — symmetric & low-skew
# ================================================
# Symmetric distributions whose analytic skew is exactly 0. The validation
# code must not flag empirical skew dust as a CV/skew failure
# (tests/test_validation.py).
agg U.Die     dfreq [1] dsev [1:6]                                                            note{fair die; analytic skew = 0}
agg U.LN     25 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 0.75 poisson                                         note{genuinely skewed; skew > 0.1 preserved}
port U.TestSym agg A dfreq [1] dsev [1:6] agg B dfreq [1] dsev [1:6]


# V. Underwriter / build smoke fixtures
# =====================================
# Single-output / multi-output / kind-dispatch coverage for ``build()`` and
# the knowledge base (tests/test_underwriter.py). PhaseZero: names match
# the pre-refactor pin so renaming is detectable.
agg V.PhaseZero:Dice  dfreq [3] dsev [1:6]                                                    note{build() returns Aggregate}
sev V.PhaseZero:S     lognorm 100 cv 1                                                        note{build() returns Severity}
port V.PhaseZero:P agg A1 1 claim sev lognorm 10 cv 1 fixed agg A2 1 claim sev lognorm 20 cv 1 fixed
distortion V.PhaseZero:D ph 0.5                                       # build() returns Distortion
agg V.PhaseZero:IP    1 claim sev lognorm 10 cv 1 fixed                                       note{_interpret_program: knowledge filled, object None}
# Multi-output: build() raises ValueError, build_many returns the list.
agg V.PhaseZero:Multi1 1 claim sev lognorm 10 cv 1 fixed
agg V.PhaseZero:Multi2 1 claim sev lognorm 20 cv 1 fixed
# discover()/describe()/plot() smoke fixtures — exercise the kind-dispatch
# in Underwriter.discover for sev and distortion.
sev        V.DiscSevTest   lognorm 100 cv 1
distortion V.DiscDistTest  ph 0.3
sev        V.DiscPlotSev   lognorm 100 cv 1
distortion V.DiscPlotDist  ph 0.3


# W. Distortion DecL constructors
# ===============================
# Combo distortions (minimum, mixture) reference child distortions by name
# via ``dist.X`` / ``distortion.X`` (tests/test_distortion_construction.py).
# Both prefixes resolve through the knowledge base.
#
# Leaf distortions are live and validated by interpret_file. The four combo
# lines below are commented out because interpret_file() parses each line
# with a fresh, empty knowledge base — combo lookups need the children
# already registered, which only happens through a sequential file load
# (Underwriter.load). They are listed verbatim for reference; uncomment when
# loading via Underwriter.load.
dist W.tcc       ccoc .25
dist W.tdu       dual 2.5
dist W.tph       ph   0.7
# Multi-parameter kinds take a FLAT number list (no brackets); the kind ->
# parameter-name mapping lives on the Distortion subclass (decl_params), applied
# by Distortion.decl_spec. See tests/test_distortion_decl.py.
dist W.tbi       bitvar 0.9 0.99 0.5
dist W.tpw       power 0.01 1.0 2
dist W.tbe       beta 0.5 2
# dist W.tMIN      minimum dist.W.tcc dist.W.tdu                            # MinimumDistortion of two
# dist W.tMIX      mixture dist.W.tcc dist.W.tdu wts [0.6 0.4]              # explicit weights
# dist W.tMIX3     mixture dist.W.tcc dist.W.tdu dist.W.tph                 # uniform default weights (3 children)
# dist W.tMIN.full minimum distortion.W.tcc distortion.W.tdu                # full ``distortion.`` prefix also works


# X. Parser-error fixtures (intentional typos)
# ============================================
# These lines DO NOT parse — they exercise the error-report pipeline
# (tests/test_parser_errors_integration.py, tests/test_parser_errors.py).
# Excluded from the read-database loaders (they would log a parse error).
# Listed for reference only; commented so test_suite parsers skip them.
#
# agg X.Typo01 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 mixd poisson 0.5     note{"mixd" -> "mixed"; UnexpectedCharacters; suggestion in report}
# agg X.Typo02 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 + +                  note{stray punctuation; UnexpectedToken}
# agg X.Typo03 100 claims sev lognorm                               note{truncated; UnexpectedEOF}
# agg X.Typo04 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 ?                    note{unknown character; no suggestions}


# Y. Reinsurance reporting regressions
# ====================================
# Drive the rationalized reins reporting surface (1.0.0a19): the consistent-
# column reins_density_df, the per-stage/per-view/per-basis reins_stats_df
# (EX exact vs Est rebucketed), and the per-stage reins_describe (occurrence
# leads Gross, aggregate leads Subject). See tests/test_reins_reporting.py and
# dev/reins-reporting.md.
#
# Structural cases (consistent columns, mass, describe shape) on lognormal
# books -- one per stage combination.
agg RR.Occ  10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 0.8 so 250 xs 175 poisson                       note{occurrence only; off-grid excess attach}
agg RR.Agg  10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson aggregate net of 500 xs 750                                note{aggregate only}
agg RR.Both 100 claims 5000 xs 0 sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 3500 po 4000 xs 1000 poisson aggregate net of 2000 xs 3000   note{occurrence + aggregate}
# Bounded severities for the EX-vs-Est numeric checks: a bounded aggregate has
# a negligible FFT deficit, so linear preserves the rebucketed mean exactly and
# nearest stays within bs/2. Fractional-share off-grid cessions exercise the
# rebucketing scatter.
agg RR.OccB 10 claims sev 500 * beta 2 3 occurrence net of 0.8 so 150 xs 100 poisson                          note{bounded; off-grid occ cession}
agg RR.AggB 10 claims sev 200 * beta 2 3 poisson aggregate net of 0.7 so 300 xs 400                           note{bounded; off-grid agg cession}
# Portfolio end-to-end gcn: a ceding port (occ on A, agg on B, gross C) and a
# gross-only control (reins objects return None).
port RR.Port agg A 100 claims 5000 xs 0 sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 2000 xs 1000 poisson agg B 50 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson aggregate net of 1000 xs 2000 agg C 20 claims sev lognorm 30 cv 1 poisson
port RR.Gross agg A 10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson agg B 5 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson


# Z. Bivariate (joint ceded, net) occurrence reinsurance
# ======================================================
# Drive Aggregate.occ_bivariate (1.0.0a20): the joint law of aggregate
# occurrence ceded C and net N via 2D FFT. Each program carries occurrence
# reinsurance; the bivariate marginals reproduce the reins_density_df occ
# ceded/net aggregates and the anti-diagonal reproduces the gross aggregate.
# See tests/test_reins_bivariate.py and dev/done/reins-bivariate.md.
agg BV.Occ  10 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 50 xs 50 poisson                               note{lognormal; general deficit-carrying path}
agg BV.OccB 8 claims sev 300 * beta 2 3 occurrence net of 0.7 so 60 xs 40 poisson                            note{bounded; tightest marginal match; positive corr}
agg BV.Fix  dfreq [4] dsev [1:20] occurrence net of 6 xs 6                                                    note{fixed count; empirical-freq PGF on the 2D grid}
agg BV.Ced  8 claims sev 300 * beta 2 3 occurrence ceded to 0.7 so 60 xs 40 poisson                          note{ceded-to output view; bivariate ignores the view}


# P&L. Negative-support (signed) severity and the output window (1.0.0a21)
# =======================================================================
# Drive negative-x: a profit is a negative loss. dsev with a negative atom
# auto-signs; continuous P&L severities use the ssev keyword (the never-clamp
# sibling of sev). A signed aggregate auto-windows from build/update (x_min
# defaults to 'auto' -> a two-sided window); a number forces the origin. See
# tests/test_negative_x.py and dev/plan-negative-x-agg.md.
agg PnL.Fix  dfreq [3] dsev [-2 5] [.5 .5]                                                                   note{fixed-3; closed form support {-6,1,8,15}}
agg PnL.SD   dfreq [3] dsev [-1 1]                                                                           note{zero-mean signed; sev SD 1, agg SD sqrt(3) -- derived from ex2, not mean*cv}
agg PnL.Sym  20 claims dsev [-1 1] [.5 .5] poisson                                                           note{symmetric P&L; mean 0}
agg PnL.Lump 1e6 claims dsev [-1 10] [15/16 1/16] poisson                                                    note{near-Gaussian lump at a negative mean ~ -312500; auto window}
agg PnL.Norm 100 claims ssev 50 * norm + 10 poisson                                                          note{continuous signed (ssev) P&L severity; never clamps}
agg PnL.PmL  5 claims ssev 100 - lognorm 80 cv .2 poisson                                                    note{premium minus loss: constant - dist reflects the severity (sev mean 20)}


# PortPnL. Portfolio combine on signed (P&L) support (1.0.0a22)
# ============================================================
# Independent signed units combine onto a shared signed grid: the FFT product
# is origin-at-0 (ftagg_density is independent of each unit's x_min) and a
# single F2 roll places the total on [x_min_tot, ...). Comparable-scale units
# share one bs so the combine is exact (means add, variances add). A signed +
# ordinary mix straddles 0. Pricing columns (add_exa) are deferred -> F/S only.
# See tests/test_negative_x_port.py and dev/plan-negative-x-port.md.
port PortPnL.Pair  agg A 50 claims ssev 10 * norm + 5 poisson agg B 50 claims ssev 10 * norm - 5 poisson
port PortPnL.Mixed agg Loss 30 claims sev lognorm 20 cv 0.8 poisson agg PnL 50 claims ssev 10 * norm poisson

# ============================================================
# PnLprem. The pnl keyword: premium-minus-loss aggregates (1.0.0a23)
# ============================================================
# pnl NAME <premium> prem - <loss body> builds an ordinary loss aggregate and
# applies an aggregate-level affine wrapper PnL = premium - A (reflect + a
# single shift by the total premium). The premium is subtracted ONCE for the
# book (unlike a constant inside sev/ssev, which is per-claim). value_type is
# set to payoff. Three exposure heads: N% lr (binds to the premium), N claims,
# N loss. The premium vectorises like an agg exposure (total pnl only). A pnl
# line goes anywhere an agg does, including as a port unit. See tests/test_pnl.py
# and dev/done/plan-pnl-premium.md.
pnl PnLprem.LR     1000 prem - 70% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                           note{loss ratio form: E[loss]=700, margin 300}
pnl PnLprem.Claims 100 prem - 10 claims sev lognorm 8 cv 1.5 poisson                                         note{claims form: premium independent of the loss model}
pnl PnLprem.Loss   100 prem - 85 loss sev lognorm 8 cv 1.5 poisson                                           note{expected-loss form: E[loss]=85, margin 15}
pnl PnLprem.Vec    [100 200 100] prem - .8 lr [1000 2000 5000] xs 0 sev lognorm 500 cv 2 poisson             note{vector premium: shift 400, mean 80 = sum premium_i (1-lr)}
port PnLprem.Book  pnl Cat 2000 prem - 55% lr sev lognorm 500 cv 3 poisson pnl Prop 5000 prem - 65% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson
# Signed loss severity (dsev negative atom / ssev) under pnl (1.0.0a45): the
# loss convolves on its genuine signed grid before the affine relabel. See
# tests/test_pnl.py and dev/done/plan-pnl-signed-severity.md.
pnl PnLsigned.Dsev   5 premium - dfreq[3] dsev[-1 1]                                                          note{signed loss: PnL in {2,4,6,8}, mean 5, sd sqrt3}
pnl PnLsigned.Asym   10 premium - dfreq[2] dsev[-2 1 3] [.5 .3 .2]                                            note{non-symmetric signed dsev: mean = 10 - 2 E[X] = 10.2}
pnl PnLsigned.Ssev   100 premium - 5 claims ssev 20 - lognorm 10 cv 0.5 poisson                              note{continuous signed severity: E[loss]=50, margin 50}

# ============================================================
# MV. The multivariate keyword: copula-coupled bivariate aggregates (1.0.0a24)
# ============================================================
# multivariate NAME <shared-count> <two agg/pnl components> copula KIND P [freq]
# couples the two components' per-claim severities by a copula and accumulates
# them with a shared outer frequency via a 2D FFT. Each component's dfreq [0 1]
# [p0 p1] is the per-event trigger; its aggregate is the per-event severity
# g_i = (1-p_i) delta_0 + p_i f_i. Marginalising one axis reproduces that
# component's standalone aggregate. A pnl component contributes its loss
# severity and applies the premium as a per-axis affine after the FFT (the
# loss-loss copula dependence becomes profit-loss). Copulas (aggregate.copula):
# normal (Pearson rho), gumbel (Kendall tau), clayton (Kendall tau), fgm
# (Spearman rho_s), independent; t is deferred. See tests/test_multivariate.py
# and dev/plan-multivariate.md.
# Programs are one-per-line (DecL is whitespace-insensitive within a program).
multivariate MV.Gumbel 25 claims agg Wind dfreq [0 1] [.3 .7] sev lognorm 40 cv 1.2 agg Flood dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 60 cv 1.5 copula gumbel 0.4 poisson
multivariate MV.ClaytonMixed 25 claims agg Wind dfreq [0 1] [.3 .7] sev lognorm 40 cv 1.2 agg Flood dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 60 cv 1.5 copula clayton 0.3 mixed gamma .2
multivariate MV.Normal 25 claims agg A dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 agg B dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev gamma 50 cv 1.0 copula normal 0.5
multivariate MV.PnLAxis 25 claims agg A dfreq [0 1] [.3 .7] sev lognorm 40 cv 1.2 pnl B 900 prem - dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 60 cv 1.5 copula gumbel 0.4 poisson
multivariate MV.Indep 25 claims agg A dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 agg B dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev gamma 50 cv 1.0 poisson
# netceded MODE: the joint (ceded, net) occurrence law of one reinsured aggregate
# (same object as Aggregate.occ_bivariate()). Body is an ordinary agg carrying
# occurrence reinsurance; the two axes are Ceded and Net.
netceded agg MV.NetCeded 8 claims sev 300 * beta 2 3 occurrence net of 0.7 so 60 xs 40 poisson

# ============================================================
# HINTS. note{} is pure text; hints{} carries key=value; build settings (1.0.0a25)
# ============================================================
# `note{...}` is free-text annotation only; `hints{...}` carries build/update
# settings (`key=value;`) parsed by the underwriter under caller-wins rules
# (explicit build() kwargs override in-program hints). note{} and hints{} are
# both optional and order-free, at most one of each. Unknown hint keys warn and
# are dropped; a duplicate key warns and the last value wins; `=`-bearing prose
# in a note no longer crashes the build. See tests/test_hints.py.
agg HINT.LogTwo  5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 2 poisson hints{log2=12}
agg HINT.Frac    1 claim sev gamma 50 cv 0.1 fixed hints{bs=1/64; log2=10}
agg HINT.Both    5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 2 poisson note{just prose} hints{log2=12}
agg HINT.HintsFirst 5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 2 poisson hints{log2=12} note{just prose}
agg HINT.ProseEq dfreq [3] dsev [-2 5] [.5 .5] note{closed form support -6,1,8,15; needs x_min<=-6}
agg HINT.Unknown 5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 2 poisson hints{frobnicate=3}
agg HINT.Dup     1 claim sev gamma 50 cv 0.1 fixed hints{bs=1; bs=2}

# ============================================================
# DISC. Discrete severity backed by an honest frozen discrete RV (1.0.0a26)
# ============================================================
# `dsev`/`fixed` severities now use `_DiscreteRV` (exact step cdf/sf, pdf=0,
# exact ppf/isf/support) instead of the old rv_histogram epsilon-jump hack.
# Every discrete moment -- unlimited, limited, or layered -- is an exact finite
# sum over the atoms, so a discrete severity never routes its moments through
# numerical isf-integration. See tests/test_discrete_severity.py.
agg DISC.Dice  dfreq [1] dsev [1:6]
agg DISC.Layer 3 claims 5 xs 3 dsev [1 4 8] [.5 .3 .2] fixed
# Off-grid atoms: with a non-integer bs these miss the grid, so the
# `dsev_bucket` placement scheme matters. `dsev_bucket='linear'` (default,
# a build/update kwarg -- not DecL syntax) splits each atom's mass across its
# two bracketing buckets to preserve the mean exactly; `'nearest'` snaps and
# biases by <= bs/2. See tests/test_dsev_bucket.py.
agg DISC.OffGrid dfreq [1] dsev [0.3 1.7 2.4] [.5 .3 .2]

# ============================================================
# TAIL. Ordered tail-thickness classification (1.0.0a29)
# ============================================================
# Deterministic family lookup gives each aggregate an ordered tail rung
# (bounded < super-exponential < exponential < subexponential < power-law) and
# a log-concave flag, surfaced via `.tail_class`, `.tail_description`,
# `.tail_explanation`, and `.info`. `.bounded` is now derived (bounded iff the
# aggregate tail class is BOUNDED). The aggregate rung is `max(freq, sev)`.
# See tests/test_tail.py and src/aggregate/tail.py (Phase 1; numeric estimator
# for histogram/meta/spliced families is deferred to Phase 2).
agg TAIL.Subexp   100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson
agg TAIL.Expon    100 claims sev gamma 100 cv 0.5 poisson
agg TAIL.PowerLaw 100 claims sev 100 * pareto 3 poisson
agg TAIL.FreqDriven 100 claims sev 100 * uniform poisson
agg TAIL.Bounded  dfreq [2] dsev [1 2 3]
agg TAIL.Mixed    10 claims sev [100 200] * [lognorm pareto] [2 3] wts [.5 .5] poisson

# ============================================================
# GH. Exponential-tilting pedagogy (Grübel-Hermesmeier, 1.0.0a46)
# ============================================================
# The Poisson/Levy aliasing demo reproduced by
# aggregate.pedagogy.gh_tilting_exhibit / tilted_aggregate_density. Tilting is a
# teaching device only; the production convolution is tilt-free (padding is the
# operational aliasing control). See tests/test_pedagogy_tilt.py and
# docs/2_user_guides/problems/010_gh_example.rst.
agg GH.PoissonLevy 20 claim sev levy poisson                                                                 note{zero-parameter Levy severity; alpha=1/2 stable aggregate}

# ============================================================
# APX. Method-of-moments approximation keyword (1.0.0a47)
# ============================================================
# `approximate sgamma | slognorm` replaces the freq x sev convolution, at
# construction, with a single continuous severity fitted to the aggregate's
# first three moments (shifted gamma / shifted lognormal; normal as the
# symmetric limit; a reflected fit for left skew), carried on a fixed frequency
# of 1. `exact` (or omitting the clause) is the inert default. Incompatible with
# occurrence reinsurance (the fit bypasses the per-occurrence convolution);
# aggregate reinsurance rides along. See tests/test_approximate.py and
# dev/done/plan-approximate.md.
agg APX.RightSgamma 5000 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson approximate sgamma
agg APX.RightSlognorm 5000 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson approximate slognorm
agg APX.LeftReflect 1 claim sev 100 * beta 5 1.3 fixed approximate sgamma
agg APX.AggReins 100 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson aggregate net of 100 xs 0 approximate sgamma
pnl APX.PnL 600000 prem - 5000 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson approximate sgamma
